      <editorialDecl>
        <correction>
          <p>Occasional correction of errors if they prevented the detection of speakers and interjections during corpus preparation. This comprised the correction of OCR errors, typos and obvious mistakes (such as wrong parliamentary group assignments) in the initial plenary protocols. Errors which had no effect on the identification of separate utterances in continuous text were not addressed systematically.</p>
        </correction>
        <normalization>
          <p>Normalization of white space characters. Table of Contents, appendices as well as header lines, footer lines and margins were removed. Some special characters such as bullet points were replaced with white space. Ellipses were replaced by three ordinary full stops. Superfluous white space characters were removed.</p>
        </normalization>
        <hyphenation>
          <p>Line breaks were resolved and hyphens at the end of lines were removed via an heuristical approach where possible.</p>
        </hyphenation>
        <quotation>
          <p>Quotes in continuous text are kept as-is and are not marked up specifically. Special quotation marks were not normalized systematically but occasionally replaced with a standard quotation mark when encoding issues were encountered.</p>
        </quotation>
        <segmentation>
          <p>Protocols were segmented into speeches and paragraphs. Up until the 18th legislative period, individual speeches were identified based on regular expressions. Starting with the 19th legislative period, the segmentation is provided in the raw data. Segmentation of speeches into paragraphs is based on an heuristical approach up until the start of the 13th legislative period. Thereafter, in most protocols (those based on plain text files and XML), segments correspond to paragraphs already found in the raw data. Paragraph segments might be modified or interrupted when necessary to identify speakers or transcriber comments.</p>
        </segmentation>
      </editorialDecl>
